Sending a signal to one thread, with a C library that is not glibc.

gdb stops and wakes threads with syscall(__NR_tkill, lwpid, signo). The
signal number is the C library's, and syscall() passes it to the kernel
unchanged -- which is only right if the two number signals alike.
picolibc does not (its SIGSTOP is Linux's SIGCHLD), so with picolibc
the call goes through tgkill(), which translates.

<asm/ptrace.h> is included only for PTRACE_GET_THREAD_AREA, which this
kernel does not have; the include is made conditional on the header.

diff -ru a/gdb/linux-nat.c b/gdb/linux-nat.c
--- a/gdb/linux-nat.c	2026-10-01 00:49:27.923347205 -0600
+++ b/gdb/linux-nat.c	2026-10-01 00:49:27.958918017 -0600
@@ -1838,7 +1838,13 @@
   int ret;
 
   errno = 0;
+#ifdef __PICOLIBC__
+  /* This C library numbers signals its own way, and syscall () would
+     hand the kernel this one's number for Linux's; tgkill translates.  */
+  ret = tgkill (linux_proc_get_tgid (lwpid), lwpid, signo);
+#else
   ret = syscall (__NR_tkill, lwpid, signo);
+#endif
   if (errno == ENOSYS)
     {
       /* If tkill fails, then we are not using nptl threads, a
